#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082942 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205ecbfe-49fd-559f-a4e7-a36900f1d9c7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "17f80459-f98c-5f34-acd4-6407c047615e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14835" :question/problem "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=2x^2-12x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=2$ and $b=-12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-12)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-12)}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e00837a5-acbf-563d-864a-2902fc8e02a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "f4cf6319-af75-53c4-a5a8-87478b7c3fd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14836" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-x^2+4x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-1$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{4}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=2.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}+4x+1 \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+8+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(2,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1ed6e03-7d4f-5606-ab01-3bfdd855348e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Coefficient Given the Axis of Symmetry"}] :question/id #uuid "dad0da4b-af03-507c-9549-6f5963783da6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14837" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=0.25x^2+kx+2$ is $x=-6.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $-6.$\n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=0.25$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(0.25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{0.5} \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-6,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e00837a5-acbf-563d-864a-2902fc8e02a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex of a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7f23c9-0bd5-5ec8-8fd7-e875fc1806da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,47)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560390} {:answer/id #uuid "317b6ffc-ad6b-5f25-a9ae-7a7a14382ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,54)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560472} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9465f1-3630-5899-98a2-9ffd8b1c2a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,47)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560598} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe77f59-59d1-5211-8384-6829442bb7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560749} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7569b9-f881-53b5-8879-4445f0b56f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-133)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e9465f1-3630-5899-98a2-9ffd8b1c2a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,47)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560598} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5e1f01-10ee-5e1a-99dc-d1ce7db0a46a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4514a54-7bd5-5a89-8cc0-e28359cd784d" :question/math-academy-id "q-10324" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-5{x}^{2}+30x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-5$ and $b=30$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{30}{2(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{(-10)} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5{x}^{2}+30x+2 \\\\  & =-5(3)^{2}+30(3)+2 \\\\  & =-45+90+2 \\\\  & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(3,47).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205ecbfe-49fd-559f-a4e7-a36900f1d9c7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola"}] :question/